No, it's just that I kept at it until I did ascend. Played for a few
years 1990-1993, went away until 2001, started playing seriously in
1999-2001, did some scumsaved games, took another break, then got
serious and played for real.  Started in 2003, YAFAP a real ascension
in 2004 -- Gnomish wizard named Shoshona.

In my case, it's not wunderkind, it's just persistence, reading lots
of spoilers, lots of practice, and a certain amount of cunning, which
is what keeps me in the game.

Keys to success?

1)  Your pet, your friend.
This is the first key to success, especially if you're playing as a
wizard or a tourist. Your pet has all the melee skills you lack.
Learn how to herd him down corridors.  I get him to fight 50% of the
monsters in the early part of the dungeon. This allows him/her to
level, making him more dangerous and ensuring he will survive if he
finds a falling rock trap -- which kills many of my early pets that
die.

Your pet will save your life.  If you're attacked by a swarm of killer
bees, engrave "Elbereth" and stand still while your pet kills them
all. Or get them in a corridor, put your pet between them and you,
then wait for a few  turns until they're all dead.

Quite apart from being a bodyguard, a pet will not step on a cursed
object .. and if it does so will do so 'reluctantly'. This allows you
to determine the bless/curse status of armor, rings, and weapons,
allowing you to get good ones of each.

Your pet will make the difference between your survival and your
death.

Which brings us to key #2:

2) Be ready to tame animals.
cats, dogs and horses can be lethal opponents.   Fortunately, they are
all tameable. This is a double victory, since you not only neutralize
your opponent but you bring him over to your side as well.   I use
tripe rations as 'charm cat or dog', and any kind of vegetable as
'charm horse'.  This ensures a ready supply of pets. Which in turn
ensures your survival.

3) Use Ranged Weapons.
Whatever your class is, find out which ranged weapons it can use, then
collect and use them.  Any class that can go beyond basic in 'dagger'
should use
dagger as combined melee and range weapon ... the stack can do
terrible damage, especially when enchanted to high values.  Also,
unlike arrows or crossbow bolts, daggers won't be destroyed by use.

If you're restricted in dagger, bow and arrows is an excellent choice,
because there are so many creatures that use bow and arrows in the
dungeon, and also many arrows traps. You won't run low on ammunition,
and they're lighter
than daggers.

Below that is the crossbow. Just don't.  IME, there aren't enough
bolts in the dungeon to be worthwhile.

But ANY ranged weapon, even unskilled, is better than no ranged
weapon.
Demons can't gate in friends if you're not in melee.  Liches can't
destroy your armor, curse your inventory, or touch-of-death you at
range.  Cockatrices can't stone you at range. Nymphs can't steal from
you at range.  So if you learn to engage your enemies at range, much
of the danger associated with such creatures evaporates.

4) Have early warning.

The thing that kills my characters most often is when I'm just running
through the dungeon and bump into something that kills me.

This is the reason my earliest ascensions were all with gnomes,
dwarves, etc. 'Cause they have infravision.  That's the easiest,
cheapest way to get
advance warning of things like floating eyes and nymphs.

Other forms of early warning include:
-- the ring of warning.  Definitely worth finding and wearing. It's
better than telepathy because it identifies monsters that are mindless
as well as other.

-- the amulet of ESP, telepathy from floating eye + blindfold.
All will show you the location of enemies out of your LOS. It's also
handy for locating pets.

-- a light source.  Magic lamp is best.

5) Be fast.
From wand of speed monster, quantum mechanic, speed boots.
If you can run away from the bad guys, that gives you more time to
shoot them with ranged weapons.

6) Use the books (when you can).
Anything you have memorized is like having a wand of near-infinite
charge. Not only is it always handy and not run out for 20,000 turns,
you can
save on carrying capacity by not needing the equivalent wands. Which
brings us to point #7)

7) Use all your wands.
If you do die in the dungeon, try to make it such that your corpse
dies with
all charges expended.  I have a lot more characters die because they
were
holding onto wands they should have used than characters die because
they'd run all their weapons dry. So if you see something really
dangerous, don't hold back!  Spam your weapons!

That's all I can think of at the moment. Does that help?

